{"name":"Liquid Nitrogen","hex":"#f3f3f4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"liquid nitrogen","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Nitrogen that has been cooled to the liquid state; used in cryogenics."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/liquid_nitrogen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Liquid nitrogen","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid nitrogen (LN2) is nitrogen in a liquid state at low temperature. It has a boiling point of about −196 °C (−321 °F; 77 K). Liquid nitrogen is produced industrially by fractional distillation of liquid air. It is a colorless, mobile liquid whose viscosity is about half that of acetone (i.e. roughly one-sixth that of water at room temperature). Liquid nitrogen is widely used as a coolant."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Liquidnitrogen.jpg/330px-Liquidnitrogen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid_nitrogen","wikidata":"Q1139370"},{"title":"Liquid Nitrogen (poetry collection)","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid Nitrogen is a collection of poems by Australian poet Jennifer Maiden, published by Giramondo Publishing in 2012."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9d/Liquid_Nitrogen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid_Nitrogen_(poetry_collection)","wikidata":"Q134360440"},{"title":"Liquid","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid is a state of matter with a definite volume but no fixed shape. When confined in a container and subject to a force such as gravity, liquids will adapt to the internal shape of the container in the direction of the force. Liquids are nearly incompressible, maintaining their volume even under pressure. The density of a liquid is usually close to that of a solid, and much higher than that of a gas. Liquids are a form of condensed matter alongside solids, and a form of fluid alongside gases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/330px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid","wikidata":"Q11435"},{"title":"Nitrogen","blocks":[{"text":"Nitrogen is a chemical element; it has symbol N and atomic number 7. Nitrogen is a nonmetal and the lightest member of group 15 of the periodic table, often called the pnictogens. It is a common element in the universe, estimated at seventh in total abundance in the Milky Way and the Solar System. At standard temperature and pressure, two atoms of the element bond to form N2, a colourless and odourless diatomic gas. N2 forms about 78% of Earth's atmosphere, making it the most abundant chemical species in air. Because of the volatility of nitrogen compounds, nitrogen is relatively rare in the solid parts of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Fluessiger_Stickstoff.jpg/330px-Fluessiger_Stickstoff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrogen","wikidata":"Q627"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Nitrogen_ice_cream_0020.jpg/500px-Nitrogen_ice_cream_0020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Nitrogen_ice_cream_0020.jpg/500px-Nitrogen_ice_cream_0020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Students preparing homemade ice cream with a dewar of liquid nitrogen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANitrogen_ice_cream_0020.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/2007TaipeiITMonth_IntelOCLiveTest_Overclocking-6.jpg/500px-2007TaipeiITMonth_IntelOCLiveTest_Overclocking-6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/2007TaipeiITMonth_IntelOCLiveTest_Overclocking-6.jpg/1280px-2007TaipeiITMonth_IntelOCLiveTest_Overclocking-6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/2007TaipeiITMonth_IntelOCLiveTest_Overclocking-6.jpg/500px-2007TaipeiITMonth_IntelOCLiveTest_Overclocking-6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Liquid nitrogen may be used for cooling an overclocked computer, when an extreme measure of cooling is needed.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2007TaipeiITMonth_IntelOCLiveTest_Overclocking-6.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Strawberry-Mint_Liquid_Nitrogen_Shake_at_HBurger_in_Denver.jpg/500px-Strawberry-Mint_Liquid_Nitrogen_Shake_at_HBurger_in_Denver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Strawberry-Mint_Liquid_Nitrogen_Shake_at_HBurger_in_Denver.jpg/1280px-Strawberry-Mint_Liquid_Nitrogen_Shake_at_HBurger_in_Denver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Strawberry-Mint_Liquid_Nitrogen_Shake_at_HBurger_in_Denver.jpg/500px-Strawberry-Mint_Liquid_Nitrogen_Shake_at_HBurger_in_Denver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A milkshake prepared with liquid nitrogen. Nitrogen vapour can be seen emanating from the top of the glass.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStrawberry-Mint_Liquid_Nitrogen_Shake_at_HBurger_in_Denver.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Liquid_Nitrogen_Tank.JPG/500px-Liquid_Nitrogen_Tank.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Liquid_Nitrogen_Tank.JPG/1280px-Liquid_Nitrogen_Tank.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Liquid_Nitrogen_Tank.JPG/500px-Liquid_Nitrogen_Tank.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Filling a liquid nitrogen Dewar from a storage tank","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALiquid_Nitrogen_Tank.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Cavitating-prop.jpg/500px-Cavitating-prop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Cavitating-prop.jpg/500px-Cavitating-prop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cavitation in water from a boat propeller","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACavitating-prop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/500px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/1280px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/500px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface waves in water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Viscosities.gif/500px-Viscosities.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Viscosities.gif/500px-Viscosities.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A simulation of viscosity. The fluid on the left has a lower viscosity and Newtonian behavior while the liquid on the right has higher viscosity and non-Newtonian behavior.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AViscosities.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG/500px-Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG/1280px-Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG/500px-Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A lava lamp contains two immiscible liquids (a molten wax and a watery solution) which add movement due to convection. In addition to the top surface, surfaces also form between the liquids, requiring a tension breaker to recombine the wax droplets at the bottom.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_Lava_lamp.JPG"}],"sounds":{}}}